# Copy to .env. Docker Compose reads it automatically, and so do the tests and `cargo sqlx`.
#
# The stack starts without it: `docker compose up --build --scale solver=4` falls back
# to the values shown here. A .env is for the tests, which need DATABASE_URL, and for
# changing a setting.

POSTGRES_USER=routeforge
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=routeforge
POSTGRES_DB=routeforge
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# Must match the four values above.
# 127.0.0.1, not localhost: on Windows with Docker Desktop, localhost resolves to ::1 first,
# and a connection to the published port hangs there for about 20 seconds before the client
# falls back to IPv4. Every test and every pooled connection would pay that.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://routeforge:routeforge@127.0.0.1:5432/routeforge

# The solver's settings, shown with their defaults. Docker Compose passes the ones that
# are set here on to every solver instance. A solver that runs outside Docker reads
# them, and DATABASE_URL, from its own environment.
#
# Workers in one process. Scaling is by instances: docker compose up --scale solver=4
#SOLVER_CONCURRENCY=1
# How long a claim holds without a heartbeat. The job of a worker that dies is taken
# over this long after its last one.
#SOLVER_LEASE_SECS=30
# How often a running job extends its lease, which is also how soon a cancelled plan
# stops its searches. At most half the lease.
#SOLVER_HEARTBEAT_SECS=5
# How often an idle worker looks at the queue without having been notified.
#SOLVER_POLL_SECS=5
# How often, at most, a route search announces how far it has got and with which
# route, in milliseconds. This is how often a line on the map moves.
#SOLVER_PROGRESS_MS=250
# `json` for one JSON object per line. The API reads these two as well.
#LOG_FORMAT=text
#RUST_LOG=info

# The API's settings. Docker Compose passes the ones that are set here on to the api
# service. An API that runs outside Docker reads them, and DATABASE_URL, from its own
# environment: it does not read .env. CLAUDE.md shows how to set them in a shell.
#
# The port of this machine the page and the API are at. Compose's alone: inside its
# container the API listens on 8080 whatever this says.
#API_PORT=8080
# The key that signs the login tokens: whoever has it can sign a token for any user
# and role. At least 32 bytes. The binary has no default for it. Compose falls back to
# the one shown, which is in the repository for everybody to read, so set your own
# before the port is opened to anything but this machine.
#JWT_SECRET=routeforge-demo-signing-key-not-a-secret
# The admin to make when the API starts, if no account has that address yet. An
# account that is there is left as it is, its password included. Compose falls back to
# these two, and they are as public as the key above.
#ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
#ADMIN_PASSWORD=routeforge
# The key the Telegram bot shows to be given a token of the user it is talking to.
# Whoever has it can be given a token of any account the bot reaches. At least 32
# bytes. The binary has no default for it, and an API without one takes no bot.
# Compose falls back to the one shown, and it is as public as the key above.
#BOT_SERVICE_KEY=routeforge-demo-bot-key-not-a-secret
# The rest, shown with their defaults:
#
# The address and port to listen on. Compose sets it to 0.0.0.0:8080 for the container.
#API_BIND=127.0.0.1:8080
# How long a login is good for. A role that an admin changes applies from the user's
# next login.
#JWT_TTL_SECS=3600
# How long a token is good for that the bot was given. The bot asks for one whenever
# it needs one, so a role that an admin changes applies to what the user asks next.
#BOT_JWT_TTL_SECS=60
# The time each route of a plan may be searched for when the plan asks for no budget,
# and the most a plan may ask for: of time, and of candidate moves.
#API_BUDGET_DEFAULT_MS=10000
#API_BUDGET_MAX_MS=300000
#API_BUDGET_MAX_ITERATIONS=10000000000
# A worker that was seen within this long counts as alive in the stats. A few times
# SOLVER_HEARTBEAT_SECS.
#API_WORKER_ALIVE_SECS=15

# The Telegram bot's settings. Docker Compose passes the ones that are set here on to
# the bot service. A bot that runs outside Docker reads them from its own environment.
#
# The token @BotFather gives for a new bot. It is the one thing the bot needs that is
# not here already: with it set, `docker compose up` brings up a bot that takes orders.
# Without it the bot says so and idles. Whoever has the token is the bot, so this one
# is a secret, and has no value to fall back on.
#TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# BOT_SERVICE_KEY, above, is the bot's as well: it shows the API that key.
#
# The rest, shown with their defaults:
#
# Where the bot finds the API. Compose sets it to http://api:8080 for the container.
#BOT_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080
# Where the Bot API is, for a Bot API server of your own. Telegram's if not set.
#TELEGRAM_API_URL=
# The zone the bot's clock is in, which decides what day "today" is on its buttons.
# A container is on UTC without it. The buttons name the day they mean either way.
#TZ=Europe/Kyiv
